       The  original  Linux system call was named ssiiggppeennddiinngg().  However, with
       the addition of real‐time signals in Linux 2.2, the fixed‐size,  32‐bit
       _s_i_g_s_e_t___t  argument  supported by that system call was no longer fit for
       purpose.  Consequently, a new system call, rrtt__ssiiggppeennddiinngg(),  was  added
       to support an enlarged _s_i_g_s_e_t___t type.  The new system call takes a sec‐
       ond  argument,  _s_i_z_e___t _s_i_g_s_e_t_s_i_z_e, which specifies the size in bytes of
       the signal set in _s_e_t.  The glibc ssiiggppeennddiinngg() wrapper  function  hides
       these  details  from us, transparently calling rrtt__ssiiggppeennddiinngg() when the
       kernel provides it.

